  "description": "Set an existing DataFrame column as the primary index and return index values and remaining column names.\n\nSetting meaningful indices provides fast hash-based record lookup and alignment across joins.\n\n### Mathematical Formulation\n$$\n\\text{df}_{\\text{idx}} = \\text{df}.\\operatorname{set\\_index}(\\text{index\\_col})\n$$\n\n### Step-by-Step Implementation\n1. Select target column as primary index identifier\n2. Transfer target column from data grid to DataFrame index structure\n3. Report index values and remaining feature columns\n\n### Returns\nDictionary with 'index_name', 'index_values', and 'remaining_columns'.",
  "theory": "## Understanding the Theory: Setting Index\n\n### Core Intuition: Why Do We Need This?\nReal-world tabular datasets are frequently messy, containing missing values, duplicates, and unaligned columns. Pandas provides powerful DataFrame primitives for reshaping, filtering, and cleaning structured data before training machine learning models. **Setting Index** performs structured data manipulation efficiently.\n\nSetting meaningful indices provides fast hash-based record lookup and alignment across joins. In this problem, the goal is to: set an existing dataframe column as the primary index and return index values and remaining column names.\n\n### Mathematical Principles & Formulation\nThe core governing equation for this operation is:\n\n$$\n\\text{df}_{\\text{idx}} = \\text{df}.\\operatorname{set\\_index}(\\text{index\\_col})\n$$\n\nEvery term in this formulation plays a precise mathematical role in ensuring dimensional consistency, numerical stability, and correct algorithmic behavior.\n\n### Step-by-Step Computational Workflow\n1. **Phase 1**: Select target column as primary index identifier\n2. **Phase 2**: Transfer target column from data grid to DataFrame index structure\n3. **Phase 3**: Report index values and remaining feature columns\n\n### Practical Machine Learning Applications & Nuances\n* **Vectorized Array Operations**: Applying vectorized transformations operates on contiguous C memory buffers under the hood, running orders of magnitude faster than iterating with Python loops.\n* **Data Cleansing**: Handling duplicates and missing data prevents bias and data leakage into downstream machine learning models.\n* **Memory Management**: Choosing appropriate column dtypes (e.g. categorical or int32 instead of object) drastically reduces DataFrame memory usage.",
  "examples": [
    "Input: records = [{\"user_id\": 101, \"name\": \"Alice\", \"score\": 95}, {\"user_id\": 102, \"name\": \"Bob\", \"score\": 88}], index_col = \"user_id\"\n\nOutput: {\"index_name\": \"user_id\", \"index_values\": [\"101\", \"102\"], \"remaining_columns\": [\"name\", \"score\"]}\n\nExplanation: Evaluated pandas_setting_index under standard test conditions."
  ],
  "requirements": "* Function must be named `pandas_setting_index`\n* Return value must match the exact expected structure and types\n* Handle edge cases cleanly and maintain numerical stability\n* Must run efficiently without redundant memory allocations",
